**Short answer:**  
Yes – you can keep the Leaflet map you already have and “plug-in” a MapLibre GL JS vector-tile basemap as a single Leaflet layer. The core Leaflet library only knows how to draw raster tiles, but the **`maplibre-gl-leaflet`** plugin wraps a full MapLibre GL JS map inside a Leaflet layer, letting you use any MapLibre style (vector tiles, raster-dem, sprites, glyphs, etc.) together with the rest of your Leaflet layers.

Below is a complete, step-by-step guide that covers:

1. **Why you need the plugin** (Leaflet’s limitation).  
2. **Installing the required libraries** (CDN or npm).  
3. **Creating the Leaflet map and adding the MapLibre layer**.  
4. **Common configuration details** (style URL, attribution, CORS, layer order).  
5. **Mixing Leaflet and MapLibre layers** (markers, pop-ups, interaction).  
6. **Performance & deployment tips** (tile source choice, caching, offline).  

---

## 1️⃣ Why a plugin is required

| Library | What it can render | Typical use case |
|---------|-------------------|------------------|
| **Leaflet core** | Raster tiles (XYZ, WMTS, WMS) | Simple web maps, low-bandwidth apps |
| **MapLibre GL JS** | Vector tiles, raster-dem, raster imagery, images, videos | Full-style, interactive, resolution-independent basemaps |
| **maplibre-gl-leaflet** | Embeds a MapLibre GL JS map *as a Leaflet layer* | Gives you the best of both worlds |

Leaflet cannot directly consume a `vector` source because it does not have a WebGL renderer that understands the Mapbox Vector Tile (MVT) format. The plugin creates a hidden `<canvas>` that runs MapLibre GL JS, then synchronises the Leaflet view (center, zoom, bearing, pitch) with that canvas. From the outside it looks like any other Leaflet layer.

---

## 2️⃣ Install the libraries

You have two main ways to bring the code into your project.

### A. CDN (quick prototype)

```html
<link
  rel="stylesheet"
  href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css"
/>
<link
  rel="stylesheet"
  href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css"
/>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/dist/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>
```

> **Version note:** As of 2026-11-04 the latest stable releases are Leaflet 1.9.4, MapLibre GL JS 4.2.2, and `maplibre-gl-leaflet` 0.0.13. Keep them in sync; the plugin works with any MapLibre GL JS ≥ 2.0.

### B. npm / bundler (production)

```bash
npm i leaflet maplibre-gl maplibre-gl-leaflet
```

Then in your JavaScript (ESM or CommonJS):

```js
import L from 'leaflet';
import 'leaflet/dist/leaflet.css';

import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

import { MapLibreGL } from 'maplibre-gl-leaflet';
```

---

## 3️⃣ Create the Leaflet map and add the MapLibre basemap

```js
// 1️⃣ Initialise the Leaflet map (any CRS you like; most basemaps use EPSG:3857)
const leafletMap = L.map('map', {
  center: [40.7128, -74.0060], // New York City
  zoom: 12,
  // optional: enable rotation/pitch if you need 3-D terrain later
  // preferCanvas: false,
});

// 2️⃣ Add a regular Leaflet tile layer (optional – e.g. for a fallback raster)
const fallback = L.tileLayer('https://{s}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', {
  attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors',
});
fallback.addTo(leafletMap);

// 3️⃣ Add the MapLibre GL layer
const mapLibreLayer = L.maplibreGL({
  // a full MapLibre style URL (hosted or self-hosted)
  style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_KEY',
  // optional: you can also pass a raw style object instead of a URL
  // style: myStyleObject,

  // If your vector tile source lives on a different domain, set CORS headers
  // or use `transformRequest` to add an Authorization header.
  // transformRequest: (url, resourceType) => ({
  //   url,
  //   headers: { Authorization: 'Bearer <token>' },
  // }),

  // Attribution handling – the plugin will forward the style's `attribution`
  // to Leaflet's control. You can also add your own static text.
  attribution: '&copy; MapTiler &copy; OpenStreetMap contributors',
}).addTo(leafletMap);
```

### What the code does

| Step | Effect |
|------|--------|
| `L.map()` | Creates the Leaflet map container and view. |
| `L.tileLayer()` (optional) | Gives you a raster fallback if the GL layer fails to load (good for offline demos). |
| `L.maplibreGL({style})` | Instantiates a hidden MapLibre GL JS map, loads the vector-tile style, and registers it as a Leaflet `Layer`. |
| `.addTo(leafletMap)` | Inserts the GL canvas into Leaflet’s overlay pane (by default `overlayPane`). |

**Result:** The vector-tile basemap renders with crisp labels, smooth zoom, and full style control, while you can still add ordinary Leaflet layers (markers, polylines, GeoJSON, etc.) on top.

---

## 4️⃣ Common configuration details

### 4.1 Style URL & Tile Source

- **Hosted style services** (MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, MapLibre-compatible Mapbox styles) already bundle a `vector` source with a TileJSON pointing at the tile endpoint (e.g. `https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/streets/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf?key=...`).
- **Self-hosted style**: Put a `style.json` on your server, reference a `vector` source that points at your own tile server (or a PMTiles endpoint). The plugin does not care where the tiles come from as long as they are reachable via HTTP(S) and CORS-enabled.

### 4.2 Attribution

Leaflet’s built-in attribution control (`L.control.attribution`) will automatically display the `attribution` field from the MapLibre style. If you need to add extra text (e.g., “Data © MyCompany”), just call:

```js
leafletMap.attributionControl.addAttribution('Data © MyCompany');
```

### 4.3 CORS & Authentication

If your vector tile endpoint is on a different origin, the server must send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or the specific origin). For token-protected services you can use the `transformRequest` hook:

```js
const mapLibreLayer = L.maplibreGL({
  style: 'https://mytiles.example.com/style.json',
  transformRequest: (url, resourceType) => {
    if (resourceType === 'Source' && url.includes('mytiles.example.com')) {
      return {
        url,
        headers: { Authorization: 'Bearer MY_SECRET_TOKEN' },
      };
    }
    return { url };
  },
});
```

### 4.4 Layer order (z-index)

Leaflet draws layers in the order they are added. The GL canvas lives in the `overlayPane` by default, which sits **above** the default `tilePane` (raster tiles) but **below** the `markerPane` and `popupPane`. If you want Leaflet markers to appear *on top* of the vector basemap (the usual case), just add them after the GL layer:

```js
L.marker([40.7128, -74.0060]).addTo(leafletMap);
```

If you need a Leaflet raster layer *under* the GL basemap (e.g., a satellite image with vector labels on top), add the raster **before** the GL layer, or explicitly set the pane:

```js
const satellite = L.tileLayer('https://sat.example.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg', {
  pane: 'tilePane', // default, renders under the GL canvas
});
satellite.addTo(leafletMap);
```

### 4.5 Interaction with the GL map

- **Map events** (`move`, `zoom`, `click`, etc.) fire on the Leaflet map as usual. The plugin forwards them to the hidden GL map, so any `map.on('click', ...)` you register on the Leaflet map will also receive clicks on vector features (if you query them via MapLibre later).
- **Feature query**: To get properties of a vector feature under the cursor you need to use MapLibre’s `queryRenderedFeatures`. The plugin exposes the underlying GL map via `mapLibreLayer.getMap()`:

```js
leafletMap.on('click', (e) => {
  const glMap = mapLibreLayer.getMap(); // MapLibre GL map instance
  const features = glMap.queryRenderedFeatures(
    glMap.project([e.latlng.lat, e.latlng.lng]),
    { layers: ['road', 'water'] } // optional filter
  );
  if (features.length) {
    console.log('Clicked feature:', features[0].properties);
  }
});
```

---

## 5️⃣ Mixing Leaflet and MapLibre layers

| Use case | Recommended approach |
|----------|----------------------|
| **Markers / pop-ups** | Use regular Leaflet `L.marker`, `L.circle`, `L.popup`. They sit in `markerPane` and appear above the GL basemap. |
| **Leaflet GeoJSON overlay** | `L.geoJSON(geojsonData, options).addTo(map)`. Works fine for < 5 k features; otherwise consider a separate vector-tile source. |
| **Heatmap / raster overlay** | Add a Leaflet `L.tileLayer` that points at a raster-tile service (e.g., a WMS or a pre-rendered heatmap). |
| **3-D terrain** | Add a `raster-dem` source *inside* the MapLibre style (e.g., `terrain: { source: 'terrainSource', exaggeration: 1.5 }`). The plugin will automatically enable terrain if the style includes it. |
| **Custom GL layers (e.g., fill-extrusion)** | Define them in the MapLibre style JSON; they will render on the GL canvas just like any other vector layer. |

**Tip:** Keep the number of Leaflet overlay panes low. Each pane creates a separate DOM element, which can affect performance on very busy maps. The default panes (`tilePane`, `overlayPane`, `shadowPane`, `markerPane`, `tooltipPane`, `popupPane`) are usually sufficient.

---

## 6️⃣ Performance & deployment considerations

| Decision | Impact |
|----------|--------|
| **Use a hosted vector-tile service** (MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, etc.) | No tile-generation infrastructure; you get global coverage, CDN caching, and automatic updates. |
| **Self-host vector tiles** (e.g., `tippecanoe` → PMTiles → `pmtiles` server) | Full control over data, styling, and licensing. Requires a tile server or static-file host that supports range requests. |
| **GeoJSON overlay vs. vector-tile overlay** | < 5 k features → GeoJSON is simplest. > 5 k → consider a small vector-tile source (e.g., `tippecanoe --maximum-zoom=14`). |
| **CORS** | Ensure your tile server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or the specific origin). Without it the GL canvas will be tainted and you cannot query features. |
| **Cache-control** | Set `Cache-Control: max-age=86400` (or longer) on tile responses to let browsers and CDNs cache them

**Short answer:**  
Yes – but not with plain Leaflet alone. Leaflet’s core can only draw raster tiles, so to show a **MapLibre-style vector-tile basemap** you need to embed a tiny MapLibre GL JS map inside the Leaflet map. The officially-supported way to do that is the **`maplibre-gl-leaflet`** plugin.

Below is a step-by-step guide, plus a few “gotchas” you’ll run into the first time you try it.

---

## 1. Why you need a plugin

| Library | What it can render out-of-the-box |
|---------|-----------------------------------|
| **Leaflet** | Raster tiles (XYZ, WMTS, WMS) only. No vector-tile decoding or style evaluation. |
| **MapLibre GL JS** | Vector tiles (or raster tiles) **and** full style JSON. Renders everything client-side. |
| **maplibre-gl-leaflet** | A thin wrapper that creates a MapLibre GL canvas as a Leaflet layer, letting you keep the rest of your Leaflet UI (markers, pop-ups, controls, etc.) while the basemap is rendered by MapLibre. |

So the workflow is:

```
Leaflet map  <--->  maplibre-gl-leaflet layer (MapLibre GL canvas)  <--->  vector-tile source + style JSON
```

---

## 2. Install the three pieces you need

You can pull them from a CDN (quick prototype) or install via npm/yarn for a production build.

### CDN (quick start)

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css" />
<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>

<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" />
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>
```

> **Version tip:** Keep the three libraries roughly in sync. `maplibre-gl-leaflet` is built against a specific major version of MapLibre GL JS; if you upgrade one, check the plugin’s `peerDependencies` and bump the other if needed.

### npm / Yarn (production)

```bash
npm i leaflet maplibre-gl maplibre-gl-leaflet
# or
yarn add leaflet maplibre-gl maplibre-gl-leaflet
```

Then import in your bundler (ESM, CommonJS, etc.):

```js
import L from 'leaflet';
import 'leaflet/dist/leaflet.css';

import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

import { MapLibreGL } from 'maplibre-gl-leaflet';
```

---

## 3. Create the Leaflet map **first**

```js
// Choose a CRS that matches the vector tiles (Web Mercator = EPSG:3857)
const leafletMap = L.map('map', {
  center: [40.7128, -74.0060], // New York City
  zoom: 12,
  // optional: set maxZoom/minZoom to match your tile source
  maxZoom: 18,
  minZoom: 0,
});
```

Add any Leaflet-only layers you already have (markers, GeoJSON overlays, etc.) **before** you add the MapLibre layer, or you’ll have to manage ordering later.

---

## 4. Add the MapLibre GL layer as a Leaflet overlay

```js
// 1️⃣  Choose a style URL that points at a MapLibre-compatible style JSON.
//    You can use a public provider (MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, etc.) or host your own.
const styleUrl = 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_API_KEY';

// 2️⃣  Create the MapLibreGL Leaflet layer
const glLayer = new MapLibreGL({
  // Pass the style URL (or an inline style object)
  style: styleUrl,

  // Optional: if you need to add your own sources on top of the basemap,
  // you can do it here via the `maplibreOptions` hook.
  // Example: add a GeoJSON source after the MapLibre map is created.
  //   maplibreOptions: (map) => {
  //     map.addSource('my-data', { type: 'geojson', data: '/data.geojson' });
  //   },

  // The plugin creates a MapLibre GL map internally; you can forward any
  // MapLibre GL options you need (e.g., `hash: true`, `preserveDrawingBuffer`).
  maplibreOptions: {
    // keep the same center/zoom as the Leaflet map (the plugin syncs them)
    // but you can also set a different initial view if you like.
  },

  // If you want the MapLibre canvas to be *below* Leaflet vector layers,
  // set `zIndex` lower than those layers. Default is 0 (above the base tile layer).
  // Example: put it under a Leaflet marker pane:
  //   pane: 'tilePane',
});
```

### Add it to the Leaflet map

```js
glLayer.addTo(leafletMap);
```

That’s it – the vector-tile basemap now appears under (or over) your existing Leaflet layers, depending on the `pane`/`zIndex` you chose.

---

## 5. Keep the two maps in sync (the plugin does this for you)

`maplibre-gl-leaflet` automatically:

* **Synchronises view state** – when the user pans/zooms the Leaflet map, the internal MapLibre GL map receives the same `center`, `zoom`, `bearing`, and `pitch`.
* **Propagates events** – click/hover events on the MapLibre canvas are re-emitted as Leaflet events (`click`, `mousemove`, etc.) on the layer, so you can attach listeners just like any other Leaflet layer:

```js
glLayer.on('click', (e) => {
  // e.originalEvent is the native mouse event
  // e.latlng is the geographic coordinate
  console.log('Clicked on basemap at', e.latlng);
});
```

If you need **feature queries** (e.g., “what road am I clicking on?”), you can reach into the underlying MapLibre map:

```js
glLayer.getMap().queryRenderedFeatures(
  glLayer.getMap().project(e.latlng), // point in pixel coordinates
  { layers: ['road-primary', 'road-secondary'] } // optional filter
);
```

---

## 6. Adding your own data on top of the basemap

You have three common patterns:

| Pattern | Where the data lives | Typical use-case |
|--------|----------------------|-----------------|
| **Leaflet GeoJSON layer** | Leaflet (`L.geoJSON`) | Small (< 5 k features) interactive data; you already have Leaflet UI for pop-ups. |
| **Leaflet vector overlay (e.g., `L.circleMarker`)** | Leaflet | Simple point symbols, heat-maps, etc. |
| **MapLibre vector source** | Inside the MapLibre GL map (via `maplibreOptions` or later `glLayer.getMap().addSource`) | Larger datasets that you want to style with the same GL style language, or you need feature-querying on the same canvas. |

**Example: Adding a GeoJSON overlay as a Leaflet layer**

```js
fetch('/data/points.geojson')
  .then(r => r.json())
  .then(geojson => {
    L.geoJSON(geojson, {
      pointToLayer: (feat, latlng) => L.circleMarker(latlng, { radius: 6, fillColor: '#ff6600', color: '#fff', weight: 1, opacity: 1, fillOpacity: 0.8 })
    }).addTo(leafletMap);
  });
```

**Example: Adding a vector source to the MapLibre canvas**

```js
glLayer.getMap().on('load', () => {
  glLayer.getMap().addSource('my-polygons', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: '/data/polygons.geojson'
  });

  glLayer.getMap().addLayer({
    id: 'my-polygons-fill',
    type: 'fill',
    source: 'my-polygons',
    paint: {
      'fill-color': '#0080ff',
      'fill-opacity': 0.4
    }
  });
});
```

> **Tip:** If you go the MapLibre-source route, remember that the source lives **inside** the GL map, not the Leaflet map. It will be rendered *below* any Leaflet vector layers unless you adjust the `pane`/`zIndex` of the `glLayer`.

---

## 7. Attribution & CORS

* **Attribution** – The style JSON usually contains an `attribution` field. The plugin copies it into a Leaflet control automatically, but you can also add your own:

```js
L.control.attribution({ prefix: false })
  .addAttribution('© OpenStreetMap contributors')
  .addTo(leafletMap);
```

* **CORS** – Vector-tile services must allow cross-origin requests (or you must serve them from the same origin). If you see `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` errors in the console, either:
  * Enable CORS on the tile server,
  * Use a proxy (e.g., Cloudflare Workers, Netlify Functions),
  * Or switch to a provider that already serves CORS-enabled tiles (MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, etc.).

---

## 8. Performance considerations

| Factor | Impact | Mitigation |
|--------|--------|------------|
| **Number of Leaflet layers** | Each Leaflet vector layer adds its own DOM/Canvas overhead. | Keep Leaflet overlays lightweight; use MapLibre sources for larger datasets. |
| **MapLibre GL canvas size** | The GL canvas is a single WebGL surface, very efficient. | No extra work needed; just avoid unnecessary `setStyle` calls. |
| **Tile cache** | Browsers cache raster tiles automatically; vector tiles are cached by the GL library. | Set appropriate `Cache-Control` headers on your tile server. |
| **Device GPU** | Mobile devices with weak GPUs may struggle with heavy GL styles (many symbol layers, 3-D terrain). | Simplify the style (fewer symbol layers, no 3-D extrusions) for mobile, or serve a separate “mobile style”. |

---

