**Short answer:** Yes. Leaflet itself can only draw raster tiles, but you can embed a full MapLibre GL JS map (which consumes vector-tile sources) inside a Leaflet map by using the **`maplibre-gl-leaflet`** plugin. The plugin creates a Leaflet layer that internally runs a MapLibre GL map, so you get a vector-tile basemap together with any other Leaflet layers you already have.

Below is a step-by-step guide, plus the “gotchas” you’ll run into the first time.

---

## 1. Why you need a plugin

| Library | What it can render natively | What you want |
|---------|----------------------------|---------------|
| **Leaflet** | Raster tiles (XYZ, WMTS, WMS) | **Vector-tile basemap** (styled client-side) |
| **MapLibre GL JS** | Vector tiles, raster tiles, raster-DEM, etc. | All of the above, plus style-driven rendering |

Leaflet’s core does **not** understand the `vector` source type, so you cannot point a Leaflet `TileLayer` at a `*.pbf` tile URL. The `maplibre-gl-leaflet` bridge creates a *single* Leaflet layer that internally runs a MapLibre GL map, which *does* understand vector tiles. The rest of your Leaflet UI (markers, pop-ups, controls, other raster layers) continues to work unchanged.

