## 4. Add the MapLibre GL layer as a Leaflet layer

```js
// 1️⃣  Choose a vector-tile style URL.
//    • Hosted style URL (e.g. from MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, or your own TileJSON)
//    • Or an inline style object (see MapLibre style spec)
const styleUrl = 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_KEY';

// 2️⃣  Create the GL-Leaflet layer
const glLayer = L.maplibreGL({
  // Pass the style URL or an entire style object
  style: styleUrl,

  // Optional: if you need to add your own sources on top of the basemap,
  // you can do it here via `addControl` or later with `maplibreGL.getMap()`.
  // Example: glLayer.getMap().addSource(...)

  // You can also set `attribution` here; Leaflet will merge it with its own.
  attribution: '&copy; <a href="https://www.maptiler.com/">MapTiler</a> contributors',
}).addTo(leafletMap);
```

That’s it – the vector-tile basemap now appears under (or over) any other Leaflet layers you add.

### 4.1. Controlling layer order

Leaflet draws layers in the order they are added. If you want **your Leaflet markers to sit on top of the GL basemap**, add the GL layer **first**, then add the markers:

```js
glLayer.addTo(leafletMap);          // basemap (bottom)
L.marker([40.7128, -74.0060]).addTo(leafletMap); // on top
```

If you need a **vector overlay** (e.g. your own GeoJSON) *inside* the GL style, you have two options:

| Option | How |
|--------|-----|
| **Add a GeoJSON source to the GL map** | After the GL layer is added, call `glLayer.getMap().addSource('my-data', {type:'geojson', data:myGeojson});` then add a layer that references it. |
| **Add a Leaflet GeoJSON layer** | Simpler if you already use Leaflet for interaction; the data will be rendered as a Leaflet overlay on top of the GL basemap. |

Both work; choose the one that matches where you want the interaction logic to live.

---

## 5. Example: Adding a custom GeoJSON overlay **inside** the GL style

```js
// Assume `myGeojson` is a valid GeoJSON object (or URL)
const glMap = glLayer.getMap(); // This is the underlying MapLibre GL map

glMap.on('style.load', () => {
  // 1️⃣ Add the source
  glMap.addSource('my-data', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: myGeojson, // could also be a URL string
  });

  // 2️⃣ Add a layer that draws it (e.g., fill polygons)
  glMap.addLayer({
    id: 'my-data-fill',
    type: 'fill',
    source: 'my-data',
    paint: {
      'fill-color': '#ff6600',
      'fill-opacity': 0.4,
    },
  });

  // 3️⃣ Optionally insert it before the first symbol layer so labels stay on top
  const firstSymbol = glMap.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol');
  if (firstSymbol) {
    glMap.moveLayer('my-data-fill', firstSymbol.id);
  }
});
```

> **Why wait for `style.load`?**  
> The basemap style may already contain many layers. Adding your source/layer *after* the style is fully loaded guarantees the style’s `layers` array exists and lets you control ordering.

---

## 6. Example: Adding a **Leaflet** GeoJSON overlay (simpler)

```js
L.geoJSON(myGeojson, {
  style: {color: '#ff6600', fillOpacity: 0.4},
  onEachFeature: (feature, layer) => {
    layer.bindPopup(`ID: ${feature.properties.id}`);
  }
}).addTo(leafletMap);
```

Because the GL layer is just another Leaflet layer, the GeoJSON overlay will be drawn **on top** of the basemap automatically.

---

## 7. Handling interactions with the GL basemap

* **Click / hover on vector features** – you need to use the MapLibre GL map, not Leaflet’s `click` events.

```js
glMap.on('click', 'road', (e) => {
  const props = e.features[0].properties;
  new L.Popup()
    .setLatLng(e.lngLat)
    .setContent(`Road name: ${props.name}`)
    .openOn(leafletMap);
});
```

* **Propagating events to Leaflet** – the plugin forwards Leaflet’s `move`, `zoom`, `resize` events to the GL map automatically, so the two stay in sync.

* **Cursor changes** – you can set the cursor on the Leaflet map based on GL hover:

```js
glMap.on('mouseenter', 'road', () => {
  leafletMap.getContainer().style.cursor = 'pointer';
});
glMap.on('mouseleave', 'road', () => {
  leafletMap.getContainer().style.cursor = '';
});
```

---

## 8. Attribution & licensing

* The `attribution` you pass to `L.maplibreGL` is merged with Leaflet’s own attribution control.  
* If you use a third-party style (MapTiler, OpenStreetMap, etc.) keep the provider’s required attribution string in the `attribution` option or in the style’s `metadata.attribution`.  
* For self-hosted tiles, make sure you respect the data source’s license (e.g., OSM ODbL, CC-BY-SA).

---

## 9. Performance considerations

| Concern | What to watch for | Mitigation |
|---------|-------------------|------------|
| **Initial GL canvas size** | The GL canvas is a single `<canvas>` element that covers the whole Leaflet map. | Keep the Leaflet map container size static (or let the plugin handle resize). |
| **Tile loading** | Vector tiles are fetched on-demand; many concurrent requests can hit browser limits. | Use a tile server that supports HTTP/2 or HTTP/3, enable `Cache-Control`, and consider a CDN. |
| **Memory** | Large vector-tile layers (e.g., global landcover) can consume > 100 MB in the browser. | Limit the zoom range (`maxzoom`) in the style, or use `source-layer` filters to drop unnecessary data. |
| **Interaction latency** | Click/hover events on dense layers may feel sluggish. | Use `filter` expressions in the style to reduce feature count at low zooms, or throttle event handling. |

---

